  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cool wedge should remain in place through \nSunday to provide cloudy skies and periods of rain.\n\nA cold front will move southeastward across the North Carolina\nand Virginia border later this afternoon into tonight, while \nhigh pressure passing eastward from Canada to the New England \nstates should allow a cool northeasterly flow to wedge against \nthe eastern slopes of the Blue Ridge. As a result, cloudy skies \nwith periods of rain showers should persist through this evening\nand taper to light rain and drizzle overnight. Patchy fog \nshould also develop along the Blue Ridge tonight. Sunday will \nstay cloudy with lower chances of rain as the cold front \ncontinues southeastward towards the North Carolina coastline and\nthe cool wedge holds firm. Temperatures were lowered below the \nmodel guidance consensus through tonight and Sunday due to the \ncool and moist air mass in place.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions will return by Monday and last \nthroughout the week. Patchy frost may occur early Tuesday and \nWednesday.\n\nHigh pressure should cross the Great Lakes on Monday and\napproach the Appalachian Mountains by Monday night. The flow \nshould turn towards the northwest and eliminate the cool wedge \neast of the Blue Ridge. As the weather turns drier, dewpoints \nshould plummet into the 40s to bring more fall-like conditions. \nLow temperatures for Tuesday morning and Wednesday morning were \nnudged downward due to ideal radiational cooling expected with \nclear skies and calm winds in the center of high pressure. Some \nlocations such as the Greenbrier River Valley and Burkes Garden \nmay see lows in the mid to upper 30s, which will bring the \npotential for patchy frost. The models keep high pressure in \nplace for the remainder of the week as temperatures moderate. \nThis situation will only worsen the ongoing drought across the \nPiedmont, and it may take until the beginning of the following \nweek for rain chances to return.",
